---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ChapterNav from '../components/ChapterNav.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="National Health Assurance: Executive Hardening">
  <main>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>The threat: dismantlement without repeal</h2>
    <p class="desc">A hostile executive would not need to win an immediate
      vote to repeal National Health Assurance. It could try to make the
      entitlement fail in practice: hold back money, leave leadership posts
      empty, hollow out staffing, delay or withdraw rules, suppress performance
      evidence, decline enforcement, or let contractors and state partners
      obstruct essential systems. The first harms would appear as interrupted
      medication, unpaid providers, closed services, broken appeals, and
      missing public reports.</p>
    <div class="hardening-boundary">
      <span>Design boundary</span>
      <p><strong>Make unilateral executive hollowing visible, temporary, and
        remediable.</strong> The architecture cannot prevent Congress from
        changing the statute or a court from invalidating an authority. It can
        prevent ordinary executive inaction from quietly operating as repeal
        and can preserve lawful continuity while Congress and courts respond.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hardening-threat-grid"
      aria-label="Ways a hostile executive could try to hollow out the system">
      <article class="hardening-threat">
        <span>01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Starve lawful payments</h3>
          <p>Withhold or defer funds, delay apportionment, transfer balances,
            or pressure the payment rail to reprice certified obligations.</p>
          <small>First visible harm: pharmacies, hospitals, and clinicians go unpaid.</small>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="hardening-threat">
        <span>02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Empty the offices</h3>
          <p>Decline appointments, manufacture quorum failure, impose a hiring
            freeze, or use a reduction in force below operating floors.</p>
          <small>First visible harm: coverage, appeals, safety, and oversight clocks stop.</small>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="hardening-threat">
        <span>03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Sabotage by rule delay</h3>
          <p>Miss mandatory rule deadlines, withdraw implementing rules, or
            use new interpretations to narrow benefits and payment duties.</p>
          <small>First visible harm: the same right produces different answers or no answer.</small>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="hardening-threat">
        <span>04</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Stop enforcing the law</h3>
          <p>Ignore rights violations, staffing breaches, extraction, fraud,
            payment obstruction, or required corrective actions.</p>
          <small>First visible harm: prohibited conduct becomes the operating norm.</small>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="hardening-threat">
        <span>05</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Hide the evidence</h3>
          <p>Suppress required reports, block audit feeds, erase delay flags,
            or prevent independent bodies from seeing operational records.</p>
          <small>First visible harm: deterioration cannot be proved or challenged quickly.</small>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="hardening-threat">
        <span>06</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Break systems and partnerships</h3>
          <p>Terminate continuity contracts, let vendors retain data or code,
            obstruct state compacts, or disable the systems that move records
            and money.</p>
          <small>First visible harm: local outages cascade into national service interruption.</small>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Defense in depth</h2>
    <p class="desc">No single official, office, rule, contract, or court route
      is expected to carry the whole defense. Select a layer to see the attack
      it blocks, the continuity action that occurs without permission from the
      obstructing official, and the independent body that checks the result.</p>
    <div id="hardening-stepper" class="hardening-stepper"
      aria-label="Seven layers of executive-branch hardening"></div>
    <div id="hardening-detail" class="hardening-detail"
      aria-live="polite"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>From interference to continuity</h2>
    <p class="desc">The plan does not wait for a discretionary
      self-report from the same chain of command accused of obstruction. It
      specifies a detection-to-remedy workflow driven by clocks, logs,
      obligations, staffing records, public-data completeness, escrow status,
      and complaints.</p>
    <div class="hardening-response-flow"
      aria-label="Detection-to-remedy workflow for executive interference">
      <article>
        <span>01</span><h3>Detect</h3>
        <p>Automatic clocks and protected records identify a missed payment,
          vacancy, missing rule, suppressed report, or vendor refusal.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span><h3>Classify</h3>
        <p>Separate lawful adjustment and ordinary incident from unlawful
          delay, nonfeasance, obstruction, suppression, or legal constraint.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span><h3>Continue</h3>
        <p>Use the narrowest lawful default immediately: payment, succession,
          last-lawful rule, escrow release, offline mode, or federal step-in.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>04</span><h3>Compel</h3>
        <p>Start anti-impoundment or administrative action within 24 hours and
          notify the congressional, scorekeeping, and rights bodies.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>05</span><h3>Reconcile</h3>
        <p>Correct provisional actions without shifting error costs to
          patients, then publish the event, duration, remedy, and result.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Hardening has measurable acceptance tests</h2>
    <p class="desc">These are controlled Phase 8 targets, not claims about
      achieved performance. Independent evidence must show that the defenses
      actually work under vacancy, withholding, rule-failure, data-suppression,
      and vendor-obstruction exercises.</p>
    <div class="hardening-target-grid"
      aria-label="Executive-hardening performance targets">
      <article>
        <strong>&ge;99.5%</strong>
        <span>core spending under mandatory payment protection</span>
        <small>TPP-12.1 / SR-LAW-001</small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>&ge;98%</strong>
        <span>critical-office staffing floor compliance</span>
        <small>TPP-12.2 / SR-LAW-002</small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>&ge;99%</strong>
        <span>critical functions continuing through leadership vacancies</span>
        <small>TPP-12.3 / SR-LAW-003</small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>&le;24 hours</strong>
        <span>to initiate action after an unlawful delay flag</span>
        <small>TPP-12.5 / SR-LAW-004</small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>&ge;99%</strong>
        <span>required public data published on time</span>
        <small>TPP-12.4</small>
      </article>
      <article>
        <strong>&ge;99%</strong>
        <span>critical legal provisions covered by a tested contingency</span>
        <small>SR-LAW-009 / verification package</small>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>The design is deliberately not one chain of command</h2>
    <p class="desc">The operating department may administer coverage, but it
      cannot be the sole holder of the money, the only source of performance
      evidence, the final reviewer of rights violations, or the court that
      orders relief. The separation below makes capture of one institution
      insufficient to disable the system.</p>
    <div class="tbl-scroll">
      <table class="data hardening-authority-table">
        <caption class="sr-only">Executive attack paths, independent checks, and continuity remedies</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Attack path</th>
            <th>Operational boundary</th>
            <th>Independent check</th>
            <th>Continuity or remedy</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th>Benefit narrowing</th>
            <td>DNHA cannot rewrite the statutory entitlement.</td>
            <td>NHAC protects rights; NHASB controls scorekeeping and formulas.</td>
            <td>A1-HCAC or district-court review; continued lawful default.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Payment obstruction</th>
            <td>THDO executes certified obligations without policy repricing.</td>
            <td>CHAO receives payment and interference evidence.</td>
            <td>Deemed or provisional payment, interest, injunction, or payment order.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>False readiness</th>
            <td>DNHA and NHTCA cannot certify their own phase success.</td>
            <td>NHASB independently verifies gates and corrective actions.</td>
            <td>Hold, resize, repair, or roll back the affected implementation wave.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>Data suppression</th>
            <td>Required feeds and publication duties do not depend on operator preference.</td>
            <td>CHAO, NHAC, and NHASB receive records appropriate to their authority.</td>
            <td>Escrow release, protected reporting, standing, and expedited relief.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th>State or vendor refusal</th>
            <td>National rights and payment duties do not depend on one compact or contractor.</td>
            <td>NHASB and CHAO record the breach and activation evidence.</td>
            <td>Portable records, step-in rights, continuity contracts, and temporary federal operation.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Limits and unfinished statutory work</h2>
    <div class="hardening-limit-grid">
      <article>
        <span>Open legal design</span>
        <h3>Leadership rules still require legislation</h3>
        <p>Governing-body composition, appointment, terms, removal, quorum,
          conflicts, vacancy, and succession are left for final statutory
          design. Trump v. Slaughter now
          forecloses ordinary for-cause insulation for officers within the
          President's general administrative control; every body still needs
          an Appointments Clause and separation-of-powers opinion.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Operational proof</span>
        <h3>A fallback on paper is not enough</h3>
        <p>Each fallback still needs authority, an owner, funds, staff,
          systems, data, contracts, an activation clock, exercises, and a
          return-and-reconciliation path. Legal contingency instruments and
          recovery-time objectives remain subjects for specialized analysis.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Constitutional boundary</span>
        <h3>Hardening is not permanent entrenchment</h3>
        <p>A later Congress may amend or repeal the statute, and courts may
          constrain particular authorities. The design instead makes those
          changes formal and reviewable while severability and scoped
          fallbacks preserve unrelated coverage, payment, rights, and
          continuity functions.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="rollout-source">
      <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sources and methodology</a>.</p>
  </section>

    <ChapterNav />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/hardening-client.ts';
</script>
